Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)
Rheumatology & Immunology
Sclerodactyly, Severe Raynaud's phenomenon, Anti-Scl-70/Anti-centromere positivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-046
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severely painful, ischemic digital ulcers on the hands, accompanied by profound difficulty swallowing solid foods and rapidly worsening shortness of breath.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing agonizing digital ischemia and new-onset severe dysphagia, complicated by progressive dyspnea. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was strictly required to physically evaluate the digital ulcers for infection/impending gangrene, rule out an acute esophageal obstruction, and assess cardiopulmonary status, as delaying until standard hours posed a significant risk of irreversible tissue necrosis and severe respiratory compromise.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My fingertips have these agonizing open sores that won't heal, I can't swallow my dinner, and my chest feels incredibly tight when I walk."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of severe, cold-induced Raynaud's phenomenon. Over the past 7 days, they developed exquisitely painful, ischemic digital ulcers on the tips of the index and middle fingers. Concurrently, the patient describes severe gastroesophageal reflux and progressive dysphagia to solid foods over the last month, culminating tonight in a terrifying sensation of food sticking in the chest. Reports a 3-month history of progressive exertional dyspnea, which has noticeably worsened this week. Denies any abrupt onset of severe headache or blurred vision (screening for scleroderma renal crisis).
Functional Status: Housebound; unable to use hands for basic tasks due to the pain of the digital ulcers; resting in a chair due to dyspnea.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of digital gangrene; risk of aspiration or malnutrition due to severe dysphagia; lives alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears thin, fatigued, and is holding hands elevated and wrapped in a blanket to maintain warmth.
Physical Exam: Dermatological: Bilateral, symmetrical skin thickening and induration progressing proximally from the fingers (sclerodactyly) is profound. The skin is taut, shiny, and cannot be pinched. Facial skin is tightened, causing microstomia (restricted mouth opening) with radiating perioral skin furrows. Matted telangiectasias are visible on the face and lips. Hard, subcutaneous calcinosis cutis is palpated over the fingertips. Extremities: Active Raynaud's present. Two exquisitely tender, 3mm ischemic ulcers are present on the right index and left middle fingertips, without frank purulence or surrounding spreading cellulitis. Musculoskeletal: Tendon friction rubs are palpable on wrist movement. Respiratory: Fine, dry basilar crackles heard bilaterally (suspicious for interstitial lung disease). Cardiovascular: Loud P2 heart sound noted (suspicious for pulmonary arterial hypertension). The diagnostic tetrad is completely aligned: classic sclerodactyly, severe Raynaud's with complications, strongly predicting positive anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl-70) or anti-centromere antibodies with specific nailfold capillary drop-out upon hospital workup.
Environmental Observations: House is kept very warm; patient sipping warm water constantly.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter antacids and paracetamol with inadequate relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma) - Status: Exacerbated (Ischemic digital ulcers and progressive cardiopulmonary/GI involvement)
Differentials:

Rheumatoid Arthritis (with secondary Raynaud's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, defining presence of sclerodactyly, microstomia, and telangiectasias, which are pathognomonic for scleroderma rather than the boggy synovial hypertrophy of RA.

Esophageal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dysphagia is set against a massive background of systemic connective tissue disease (Raynaud's, sclerodactyly), making dysmotility the overwhelmingly likely cause, though formal endoscopy is required.

Primary Raynaud's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Primary Raynaud's never causes structural tissue damage (ischemic ulcers, calcinosis, sclerodactyly) or systemic internal organ involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an advanced, complicated flare of Systemic Sclerosis. The acute development of ischemic digital ulcers indicates critical microvascular failure, while the progressive dysphagia and dyspnea indicate severe systemic fibrotic involvement (esophageal dysmotility and interstitial lung disease/pulmonary hypertension). Urgent after-hours assessment was vital to physically examine the ulcers to ensure no emergency surgical debridement was required for gangrene, and to prescribe immediate, potent vasodilators to salvage the digits. Deferring care would have resulted in unmanageable, agonizing ischemic pain and potential loss of the fingertips, strictly satisfying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a rapid-acting calcium channel blocker (e.g., Nifedipine) or an alternative vasodilator to aggressively force peripheral vasodilation and promote healing of the ischemic ulcers. Prescribe a high-dose proton pump inhibitor (e.g., Pantoprazole) to manage the severe GERD and protect the esophagus. Apply sterile, non-adherent dressings to the digital ulcers. The patient requires urgent referrals to Rheumatology, Respiratory Medicine (for HRCT/Echo), and Gastroenterology.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict thermal protection: Wear insulated gloves continuously, even indoors; demonstrating that maintaining a localized microclimate physically prevents the cold-induced vasospasm that is starving the ulcers of oxygen, directly promoting healing. Dietary modification: Eat six very small, pureed meals a day while sitting completely upright; executing this specific mechanical eating strategy utilizes gravity to push food through the rigid esophagus, instantly preventing the painful sensation of food sticking in the chest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Wound care nursing referral for the digital ulcers.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to monitor the digital ulcers and arrange the extensive specialist referrals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the fingertips turn completely black (gangrene), if the ulcers start oozing thick yellow pus and the finger becomes red and hot (infection), if you suddenly cannot catch your breath while resting, or if you develop a severe, sudden headache with blurred vision (scleroderma renal crisis).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The agonizing sores on your fingers are caused by critically low blood flow, a severe complication of your scleroderma. While there is no major infection today, the tissue is starving for oxygen and requires immediate medication to open the blood vessels.
Clinical Course: The ulcers will take weeks to heal and require strict protection from the cold and trauma. The breathing and swallowing difficulties require urgent specialist testing to determine the extent of internal scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must keep your core body and your hands strictly warm. If the tips of your fingers turn black, if you develop a sudden, pounding headache, or if you feel like you are suffocating, you must seek emergency medical help immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system has caused severe scarring and narrowing of the tiny blood vessels in your fingers, completely cutting off the blood supply and causing the skin to die (the ulcers). The prescribed blood pressure medication acts to chemically force these vessels open, returning oxygen to the dying tissue. The swallowing difficulty is due to scarring of your food pipe, making it rigid.
Actionable Steps: Take the Nifedipine to open your blood vessels. Wear gloves at all times. Eat only very soft, wet foods while sitting straight up. Keep the ulcers clean and dry with the provided dressings.

